#section{Getting started}::

Stargazer schedules telescope time from a plain-text queue. A queue entry
names a target and a priority; everything else — weather windows, moon
phase, horizon masks — is computed for you. Releases live on the
#link("https://example.org/stargazer"){project page}; questions go to the
##stargazer channel (a literal #tt{##} escapes the sigil).

You will need:

#list
	#item: a queue file (indentation is #emph{tabs}, like this book's source)
	#item
		a schedule output format, one of:
		#list(style: "1")
			#item: plain text
			#item: HTML
			#item: iCal

Horizon masks are tiny expressions; because this one contains an unbalanced
brace, its inline code uses a fence: #tt|{mask { alt > 20}|.

#blockquote{Astronomy compels the soul to look upwards. — Plato}

#hr

#image("images/dish.svg" alt: "a radio dish sketched in two strokes")

#section{First light}::

This section is written with a second hanging #tt{::} #emph{inside} the
chapter's own hanging block, so it nests one level deeper and renders one
heading level down — depth comes from nesting, never from a level argument.
